    {
      "id": "study-with-friend",
      "pattern": "[Verb] + مَعَ + [Noun + suffix]",
      "exampleArabic": "أدرس مع صديقي",
      "exampleTransliteration": "adrusu maʿa ṣadīqī",
      "exampleEnglish": "I study with my friend.",
      "grammarNote": "مَعَ ('with') and حَتَّى ('until') are two more prepositions taking a genitive noun, just like فِي/عَلَى/إِلَى before them. صَدِيق ('a friend') plus the possessive suffix -ي (level 9) gives صَدِيقِي ('my friend') — the same pattern as كِتَابِي and بَيْتِي. Note مَعَ isn't related to مَعًا ('together', level 80): أَنَا وَأَنْتَ مَعًا ('you and I, together') describes a shared state, while مَعَ is what joins two things in a sentence ('I [verb] WITH X').",
      "audio": { "id": "phrase_adrusu_maaa_sadeeqi", "text": "أدرس مع صديقي" },
      "introducedInLevel": 82
    }
  ],
